      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Soak couscous in cold water for 20 minutes. Drain in a cloth and squeeze out all the liquid."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Combine lamb mince, soaked couscous, grated onion, all spices, and salt in a large bowl."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Knead the mixture firmly for 4 minutes until it becomes a smooth, slightly sticky paste. The mixture must fully combine."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "With damp hands, shape into 8 oval patties about 2cm thick. Place on a plate, cover, and refrigerate for 20 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat oil in a frying pan over medium-high heat. Fry kibbeh for 5 minutes per side until deeply golden on the outside. Do not press them down."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Serve with cold yoghurt and lemon wedges."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Kibbeh falls apart in the pan",
              "cause": "Not enough kneading or the couscous was too wet.",
              "fix": "The mixture must be fully combined and feel like a smooth paste. Squeeze the couscous drier next time. If it falls apart now, re-form and chill longer."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Outside is dark but inside is raw",
              "cause": "Heat was too high.",
              "fix": "Reduce heat to medium and cook for 6 to 7 minutes per side. Kibbeh can also be finished in a 180C oven for 10 minutes after pan-frying."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Kibbeh is one of the Levant's oldest and most contested dishes. Lebanon, Syria, Iraq, and the Palestinian territories all claim it as their own, and each country has its own regional variations. In Lebanon, kibbeh nayyeh is eaten raw at celebratory feasts, while fried and baked versions are everyday food. The dish emigrated with Lebanese and Syrian communities to Brazil, Mexico, and Colombia, where it became absorbed into local cooking traditions. More kibbeh is now eaten in Brazil than in Lebanon."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
